    {
      "s": "TechCrunch",
      "t": "Tesla starts Optimus robot production in California as profit falls 43%",
      "d": "Tesla's revenue was flat and its expenses skyrocketed as it pressed the accelerator on its Optimus robots, AI, and autonomous driving.",
      "u": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/21/tesla-stars-optimus-robot-production-in-california-as-profit-falls-43/",
      "m": "10. 21. PM 08:33",
      "ts": 1792614789000,
      "fullText": "Tesla has started production of its Optimus humanoid robot at its factory in Fremont, California, in a manufacturing space once dedicated to making electric vehicles, the company announced Wednesday. &#8220;The bots coming off this line are the first production-quality bots that we have manufactured,&#8221; Tesla wrote in its third-quarter letter to shareholders. &#8220;While we will continue to iterate on the hardware and manufacturing process, we have proven that the current manufacturing process for Optimus is suitable for a commercial product.&#8221; Tesla CEO Elon Musk said on a conference call that he considers this &#8220;early production,&#8221; and that the ramp will be slow and &#8220;somewhat bumpy because it is such a radically new product.&#8221; He nonetheless claimed Tesla will be able to make 20,000 of them per week by the end of 2027. &#8220;There&#8217;s still some things we need to tweak about the design, because you need to deliver a robot that is that is safe and reliable and has long duration, so it can&#8217;t like, wear out after a month,&#8221; he said. &#8220;And there&#8217;s still quite a lot of work to do with regard to the AI and the robot for it to be truly useful for open-ended tasks.&#8221; That forward-looking development wasn&#8217;t enough to lift the company&#8217;s otherwise middling third-quarter earnings, results that typify a company in the midst of a major bet. Tesla&#8217;s revenue was flat and its expenses skyrocketed as it pressed the accelerator on its Optimus robots, AI, and autonomous driving. The end result was a 43% drop in profits. The company’s revenue was largely driven by EV sales, which were flat year over year at $20 billion. Tesla delivered 486,532 vehicles in the third quarter, about 6,000 more than the previous quarter, but fewer than in the same period last year. Tesla’s total third-quarter revenue was $28.04 billion, a skosh below the $28.09 billion it generated in the third quarter of 2025. Tesla’s third-"
